  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis currently highlight a\nstrong mid/upper ridge centered over the central CONUS while \nupper impulses reside over both the Pacific Northwest and \nnortheast CONUS. Showers and a few rumbles of thunder were \nobserved across portions of Kansas earlier this morning along a \nweak 850-700 mb baroclinic zone. A weak outflow boundary \ncurrently spans along a rough line from Marion to Pratt. \nIsolated to scattered activity has been observed early this \nafternoon across portions of the Flint Hills amid a weakly \ncapped airmass with MLCAPE values around 1000 J/kg. This is \nexpected to continue into the early evening primarily along and \neast of the Flint Hills, though a stray shower or storm near \nI-135 can't be ruled out considering convergence along the \naforementioned outflow boundary. As the discussion earlier this \nmorning highlighted, the main concern with the strongest \nactivity continues to be strong downburst winds with progged \nDCAPE values around 1200 J/kg. Deep-layer shear around 25 kts \nshould largely preclude broader storm organization, though a \nhandful of strong to marginally severe storms are possible prior\nto sunset. A similar setup may remain in place Monday \nafternoon, as point soundings from short-term guidance highlight\nweak capping and DCAPE values around 1000 J/kg. Once again, the\nbest chances for storms appear to reside primarily along and \neast of the Flint Hills at this time.\n\nWith ridging overhead, temperatures through Tuesday will remain hot \nfor this time of year with afternoon highs forecast near/above 100 \ndegrees across the forecast area. A shift in the hot pattern looks \nto arrive late Tuesday night into Wednesday morning as a midlevel \nwave tracks east across the Northern Plains. This will work to both \nflatten the mid/upper ridge and push a cold front through the \nPlains. Mid/long range guidance continues to indicate cooler \ntemperatures and increasing rain chances as this boundary pushes \nsouth on Wednesday, and most of the area could see highs near 90\ndegrees by Thursday.\n\nA surface trough setting up over the forecast area will allow for \nstrengthening southerly winds to close out the week. These will\npromote a slight warming trend for Friday and Saturday with \nforecast highs in the mid 90s. From here, long range guidance \nhighlights a potent mid/upper shortwave ejecting into the \nNorthern Plains late Saturday night into early Sunday. This will\ndisplace another cold front southward at some point Sunday \nwhich will bring cooler temperatures and may bring widespread \nrainfall as well. Details remain a bit unclear this far out \nconsidering discrepancies between global models such as the GFS \nand Euro, with the former preferring a quicker arrival of the \nfront which would promote highs in the upper 80s to close out \nthe weekend. All told, details are likely to change as \nadditional information becomes available, so stay tuned as we \ncontinue to refine the details.",
